# 29-kpis run 2026-10-02T16:07:57Z
== fresh Postgres for events and request_log (docker compose down -v && up) ==
 Container 29-kpis-postgres-1 Healthy 
setup: employers, members, events (usage), request_log (one row per HTTP request), change_requests
setup: 120 members (Acme 60, Globex 40, Initech 20), 110 eligible
== demo: the portal (a separate process) emits events, a simulator drives 28 days of traffic, SQL computes the KPIs ==

## 1. The app emits what happened
   concept: the portal writes a usage event per member action (events) and a row per HTTP request (request_log); KPIs are SQL over those two tables, nothing is counted by hand
   [portal pid 20524] listening on :53039, every request in request_log, usage events in events
   simulated 2026-09-03 .. 2026-10-01: 712 member visits, 672 hourly health probes, 149 change requests (137 resolved by staff); incident 2026-09-17T09:00:00Z/2026-09-17T13:00:00Z; took 3337ms
   events: login=712, view_profile=639, view_contributions=393, change_started=175, change_submitted=149, change_rejected=23

## 2. A vanity count versus adoption
   concept: "logins this month" only goes up; adoption divides distinct eligible members who logged in by all eligible members, so it says who is NOT using the portal
   logins in the window: 712 (sounds like success)
   adoption: 47.3% (52 of 110 eligible members), target >= 60%
   Acme     25/55 eligible members active (45%)
   Globex   23/37 eligible members active (62%)
   Initech  4/18 eligible members active (22%)

## 3. A mean versus the p95
   concept: a mean blends the many fast requests with the few slow ones; the 95th percentile is what the slowest one in twenty members waits
   GET /contributions       397 requests  mean   50 ms  p95  354 ms
   GET /profile             639 requests  mean    5 ms  p95   11 ms
   POST /changes            179 requests  mean    5 ms  p95   11 ms
   GET /changes/new         175 requests  mean    3 ms  p95   10 ms
   POST /login              712 requests  mean    3 ms  p95    9 ms
   all member routes together: mean 13 ms, p95 13 ms (the fast pages drown the slow one, so the KPI takes the slowest route)
   the slow tail: 14 members with more than 30 years of service, whose history query is slow

## 4. A health check versus what members got
   concept: a probe on /health that never touches the database says 100% up; availability counts the member requests that actually failed, and the SLO turns the shortfall into an error budget
   health probes: 672, 100.0% answered 200
   availability:  99.48% (11 of 2102 requests failed), SLO 99.5%
   error budget:  11 failed of 10.5 allowed, remaining -5%
   09-17: 11 of 78 member requests failed (burn rate 28.2x the budget rate)

## 5. Change request funnel
   concept: each step counts sessions that reached it; the drop between steps shows where members give up (validation errors, the outage, abandoning the form)
   login                712
   view_profile         639 (-10% from login)
   change_started       175 (-73% from view_profile)
   change_submitted     149 (-15% from change_started)
   23 sessions hit a validation error at least once

## 6. Every KPI has a definition, an owner and a target
   concept: kpis.ts is the one place a KPI is defined; validate() rejects a KPI nobody owns or that has no target, before a number reaches a dashboard
   rejected: logins: no question
   rejected: logins: no owner
   rejected: logins: no target
   8 definitions valid

## 7. The KPI report
   concept: each value is the definition's SQL run over the window, compared with its target; a missed target names the owner who acts on it
   KPI                                           value  target     status  owner          detail
   Adoption                                      47.3%  >= 60.0%   MISSED  product owner  52 of 110 eligible members
   Change request task success                   85.1%  >= 85.0%   met     product owner  149 of 175 tasks
   Change request completion time (median)       169 s  <= 240 s   met     UX lead        p90 398 s
   Form error rate                               13.4%  <= 10.0%   MISSED  UX lead        23 of 172 submissions
   Latency p95, slowest route                   354 ms  <= 300 ms  MISSED  tech lead      GET /contributions, all routes together 13 ms
   Availability                                 99.48%  >= 99.50%  MISSED  service owner  11 of 2102 requests failed
   Error budget remaining                        -4.7%  >= 0.0%    MISSED  service owner  11 failed of 10.5 allowed
   Requests resolved within 3 days               86.9%  >= 90.0%   MISSED  support lead   113 of 130 due

## 8. Static dashboard
   concept: one self-contained HTML file: no script, no external library, inline SVG bars, status as text and icon (not colour alone), definitions table under the charts
   wrote out/dashboard.html: 13434 bytes, 37 bars, 8 KPI tiles, external references: 0

